  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Benign and drier weather for the foreseeable\nfuture.\n\nThe weather will be rather underwhelming for the next several days \nbut will offer a break in the repetitive rain pattern. A cold front \nis continuing its passage today with higher pressure to follow. This \nwill be a repeating theme for the rest of the week and going into \nnext week.\n\nThe surface high following today's cold front will bring in \nrelatively drier air. The center of the high will be over the Great \nLakes but will diminish in strength as surface low moves to its east \nsouthward from Canada through New England. A mid-level omega block \npattern continues to hold though its orientation is a bit wonky. The \nridge orientation is northwesterly between the northern Great Plains \nand Florida, placing the western low component over California \nand the eastern low counterpart just northeast of Maine. This \nsetup will direct shortwaves and cooler and drier air from \nCanada as far south as the Mid Atlantic. One of these shortwaves\nmoves southeastward by the weekend, sending another cold front \nthrough Virginia. By early next week, the omega block pattern \nwill have morphed and the ridge axis will have taken a more \nmeridional orientation between the Canadian Plans and \nTexas/Louisiana. The eastern low amplifies and moves \nsouthwestward as a result, with its center over New England. The\nwestern low moves further east and both lows \"squeeze\" out the \nridge late next week to eventually break down the pattern. By \nthe end of next week, the eastern low will begin to propagate \nfreely into the Atlantic Ocean.\n\nThe summary of the mid-level pattern for the next few days are \nimportant as it explains the repetitive weather pattern at the \nsurface. With the northwesterly winds from the ridge/eastern low \nalignment, there will be at least three waves of cold fronts \nfollowed by surface high pressure systems that will come into the \nregion for the next seven days. Each front will keep temperatures \nfrom increasing and keep air conditions dry. High temperatures will \nlikely be in the upper 60s to lower 80s and dew points will get as \nlow as the 40s and 50s. PWATs as low as 0.33\" and the influence of \nthe surface highs will keep precipitation chances trivial for quite \nsome time.",
